---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/klaviyo-vs-drip';
const title = 'Klaviyo vs Drip for Shopify Email: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = "Compare Klaviyo vs Drip for Shopify email, data ownership, workflow acceptance and ongoing team responsibilities.";

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "What should decide between Klaviyo and Drip?",
    "answer": "Use the same welcome, cart, post-purchase and winback fixtures. Compare the profile/event model, entry rules, suppression, queued content and operator maintenance. No list size or revenue milestone automatically decides fit."
  },
  {
    "question": "How much do 25,000 contacts cost?",
    "answer": "Request current quotes using the same stored profiles, send volume, channels, destinations and support requirements. Include seasonal peaks and operator time; no current price bands are established here."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do imported fields recreate workflow history?",
    "answer": "No equivalence is assumed. Map profile state separately from historical events and prior entry, rebuild accepted journeys and test retries, suppressions and old queue cancellation."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can either platform replace an SMS sender?",
    "answer": "Verify current channel availability, destination and plan support, permission capture, opt-outs, replies and queue ownership in the actual account before treating it as a replacement."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Klaviyo vs Drip</nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="badge">Head-to-head</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Klaviyo vs Drip for Shopify Email</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">Compare profile state, event history, flow entry and queued content before choosing a retention workspace. All merchant scenarios below are proposed exercises, not observed tests or results.</p></header>
    <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl"><TldrBox picks={[
      {"label": "Profile/event filtering", "tool": "Klaviyo", "reason": "Evaluate documented entry and later profile checks against your merchandising rules."},
      {"label": "Event-driven retention", "tool": "Drip", "reason": "Evaluate workflow triggers and branches with the person who will maintain them."}
    ]} /></div>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Choose the data model your retention owner can maintain</h2>
      <p>Klaviyo and Drip both deserve evaluation for behavior-based retention. Klaviyo’s <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115002779051" class="link">flow documentation</a> distinguishes event-based entry conditions from profile filters, which are checked at entry and again before actions. Drip’s <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424702655501-Workflows-Triggers" class="link">workflow triggers</a> define the event that brings a person into a workflow. These are useful building blocks, not proof of a measured winner. Choose Klaviyo when its profile and event filtering fits your merchandising rules; choose Drip when a retention owner can express and audit the required journeys through its workflow triggers and branches.</p>
      <h2>Keep a profile snapshot separate from an event</h2>
      <p>For a proposed outdoor gear store, record shell-jacket versus base-layer interest, signup source, wholesale status and the exact collection or product event available from the integration. A current interest field can be overwritten; a historical viewed-product event describes a particular action. Do not quietly substitute one for the other. Record event name, timestamp, product and variant identifiers, identity key and source before building welcome or browse recovery. Confirm whether anonymous activity becomes available after signup in your actual integration.</p>
      <h2>Welcome: entry, identity and the fourth email</h2>
      <p>Use popup, footer and product-page signup fixtures. Branch welcome by verified source and relevant product interest without making a discount compulsory. For a skincare label moving from Mailchimp, propose a hero SKU in email four only when the necessary permitted browsing data exists. Test an existing customer signing up again, a new address linked to a past buyer, and a subscriber with no product history. A safe fallback should show useful education rather than an empty product block. Inspect the rendered variant, image and destination URL.</p>
      <h2>Queued content is a separate acceptance test</h2>
      <p>Place a test profile in a delay before welcome email four, then change the hero SKU and template. Record which content the waiting message actually uses in each account. Also change an interest field after entry and inspect the later branch. Entry conditions, later state checks and message rendering are separate decisions; a visible canvas does not establish when content is captured. Keep a version ledger and rollback copy. Test draft, review and live settings without sending promotional messages to real customers.</p>
      <h2>Cart fixture: $214, three items, one purchase during the delay</h2>
      <p>Propose a $214 three-item cart from a first-time visitor and an initial one-hour wait; neither is a tested optimum. Include a separate cart under $100 to test an optional 10% incentive ceiling. The latter means a $90 eligible cart would receive at most $9 off; the $214 cart must not inherit a rule intended only for the smaller basket. Check wholesale exclusion, current purchase status and replay of the same checkout event. Give each cart one sender and cancellation owner. A purchase after entry must prevent stale recovery according to the rules you validate.</p>
      <h2>Coffee replenishment: 18–45 days is a hypothesis</h2>
      <p>Use bag-size properties to propose an 18–45-day reorder range, with education versus discount as a test branch. Add a 90-day consumable with three variants as a separate fixture, not a supplier capability claim. Verify date units, missing bag size, a second order before the reminder, subscription renewal, pause and cancellation. Keep the original order event distinct from today’s subscription state. Ask a new operator to explain the next message and change a delay safely; record actual effort instead of claiming a faster builder.</p>
      <h2>Winback and catalog depth without a SKU threshold</h2>
      <p>Compare full-price repeat buyers with discount-sensitive buyers, using clearly defined historical behavior rather than inferred intent. If predictive functionality is part of a Klaviyo proposal, verify eligibility and available outputs in that plan and compare it with a maintainable rule-based baseline. Neither 300 SKUs nor 1,500 variants establishes a platform boundary. Test representative products, deleted variants, out-of-stock items, identity changes and recent purchase suppression before relying on personalization.</p>
      <h2>Quote the same workload at 25,000 and 50,000 profiles</h2>
      <p>Treat 25,000 contacts and 50,000 people as quote inputs, not published price tiers. Include a 31,000-profile list with 8,000 monthly engaged recipients to ask exactly which records count toward billing. Obtain current written quotes with send allowance, overage, suppression treatment, seasonal peaks, channel destinations, carrier charges and support scope. Model twelve months plus integration and operator hours. List cleanup must retain permission and suppression evidence; deleting records to lower a bill can destroy the evidence needed to prevent re-imported opt-outs.</p>
      <h2>Four-flow review and reporting</h2>
      <p>Build welcome by first product interest, cart excluding wholesale, post-purchase split by first versus repeat order, and winback split by discount history. Score trigger accuracy, readable exclusions, queue cancellation, safe edits and operator explanation using the same inputs. Compare segment, cohort and flow-variant reporting against reconciled orders, refunds and discounts. Attributed revenue is not causal lift: an eligible randomized holdout needs adequate sample size and uncertainty. No revenue result or merchant preference is established by these exercises.</p>
      <h2>Decision and migration ledger</h2>
      <p>Klaviyo is a positive fit for teams that want to manage retention through a documented profile/event model and recurring eligibility checks. Drip is a positive fit for teams that want to own event-triggered journeys and explicit workflow logic. Neither is an inevitable graduation path. For a move in either direction, inventory fields, tags, permission records, historical events, templates, suppressions and delayed messages. Imports do not prove equivalent entry history. Rebuild a small accepted journey, cancel old queued content, deduplicate replay and verify rollback before switching ownership. An additional email product, including Sequenzy, should have a distinct demonstrated job before adding another sender.</p>
      <h2>Channel permission, replies and contribution margin</h2>
      <p>Before enabling an SMS route, verify current supplier, plan and destination support. Review live popup, checkout and keyword disclosures with the responsible reviewer; record source, wording, time, purpose, channel scope and withdrawal. Configure timezone and quiet-hour handling, frequency limits and a reply owner. Test STOP and HELP separately from email unsubscribe; do not assume a tag or CRM stage propagates suppression between senders.</p>
      <p>For a proposed $100 order with $45 product cost and $10 fulfillment, contribution before marketing is $45. A 25% discount leaves $20 before platform fees, carrier fees and three proposed texts. The result is not automatically negative: calculate the actual fees, returns and incremental orders. Review weekly caps across broadcasts and automations, opt-outs by flow, reply backlog and full-price repeat quality. Coordinate offers across channels using the subscriber’s promise and actual collision rules rather than a universal twenty-four-hour rule.</p>
      <ul><li>Check current purchase, subscription, channel permission and suppression before dispatch.</li><li>Assign one owner for sender selection, replies, queue cancellation and cross-system suppression tests.</li><li>Re-read live disclosures when forms or channel purposes change; retain an audit trail.</li><li>Measure cost per incremental order with an appropriate holdout, uncertainty and adequate samples.</li></ul>
    </article>
    <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">Klaviyo vs Omnisend</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-drip">Sequenzy vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-mailchimp">Drip vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/supplements">Email apps for supplements</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
  </div></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Klaviyo vs Drip FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
